{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7ESxua279R4mfnSQiEi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before I draft this, a few questions. Your answers will change what the letter says:\n\n1. **The 5-week absence and the written English.** Do you want either one in the letter? My suggestion: mention the English along with the night class, since the college will see his writing anyway and the night class reflects well on him. I'd leave out the absence unless the college asks about gaps or reliability. A family emergency, plus 2 missed shifts in 3 years, is a strong attendance record. What's your call?\n\n2. **How he learned the software.** Did he take it on himself, or did you assign it? And do you know what catching those 9 mastitis cases early saved you, or what the vet said about it? Lost milk, treatment costs, cows you kept, anything like that? A concrete outcome is the most convincing thing a reference letter can include.\n\n3. **What he wants after the program.** Does he plan to come back to your farm or go somewhere else in dairy? Would you hire him again, or promote him, once he finishes? \"I would hire him back\" carries a lot of weight with an admissions reader.\n\n4. **Anything else you've seen yourself.** Has he trained newer workers, fixed a problem nobody asked him to fix, or been trusted with something beyond milking, like keys, calving, or equipment? Even one short story helps.\n\n5. **The details.** I need his full name, your name, the farm's name and location, and who the letter goes to (an admissions committee or a named person). Also let me know if the college gave you a form or specific questions to answer.\n\nIf you don't know something, just say so and I'll write around it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":256,"cache_read_input_tokens":9516,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_toke