{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7EUeMupMTUPg9G8Kupf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here's the letter. A few choices I made:\n\n- **The failed safety test is not in the letter.** A failed written test would make the committee worry about his writing even more, and the night class already answers that worry. If you'd like to include it, one honest sentence would work: \"He failed the written part of a dairy safety course once, studied, and passed it the second time.\"\n- **The 5-week absence isn't mentioned.** But I did add one general line saying he finds his own cover when he needs time off. That's true because of what he did with his cousin, and it shows he's reliable without getting into the leave.\n- **I didn't say who started the software work**, since you're not sure. The letter just says he learned it.\n- **I left out a date and address block** because it's going through the portal. If the portal asks for a signed PDF, add the date and your signature at the bottom.\n\n<final_document>\nDear Admissions Committee,\n\nI am the farm manager at Hollis Dairy, a 400-cow operation, and I am writing to recommend Tomás Reyes for your two-year agricultural technology program. Tomás has worked for us for three years, since he was 19. He is one of the most dependable people I have employed.\n\nFive days a week, Tomás runs our 3 a.m. milking shift on his own. There is no supervisor on the farm at that hour. I trust him with the whole herd and the parlor, and he has never given me a reason not to. In three years he has missed two shifts. When he has needed time off, he has arranged cover for his shifts himself before he came to me.\n\nWhat makes him a strong fit for your program is the work he has done with our herd management software. Tomás learned the system and now uses it to flag sick cows for our veterinarian. Last winter he caught nine cases of mastitis early, and our vet estimates that saved the farm about $8,000. He watches the animals, reads the data, and connects the two. That is the kind of work your program trains people to do, and he is already doing it.\n\nI'll be direct about one area where he is still growing. Tomás's spoken English is good, but his written English is weaker. He knows it, and he is doing something about it: he is taking a night English class on his own time, on top of a shift that starts at 3 a.m. That tells you how seriously he will take his coursework.\n\nTo support him while he studies, I will keep his job at Hollis Dairy open part-time on weekends for the length of the program.\n\nI recommend Tomás without reservation. If you have any questions, please call me at 555-0187.\n\nSincerely,\n\nMike Hollis\nFarm Manager, Hollis Dairy\n</final_document>"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1137,"cache_read_input_tokens":9772,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1137},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ce80a90-7cfb-4ae8-bcab-d7d07c5f3eb9","uuid":"a6670601-f89a-4c80-8bda-c87309804cab","timestamp":"2026-11-16T20:25:00.847Z","request_id":"req_011Ch7EUeA1ZFi9vuU63cUVW"}
